package modfile

// Package modfile parses tack.mod, the root file that marks a module's
// root directory and names the module. It is a port of the Go compiler's
// package modfile, function by function, and accepts and rejects exactly
// what Go's does, with the same diagnostics at the same places. The
// format is documented in full in Go's modfile/doc.go: a line-oriented
// file whose one directive, module, names the module once.
//
// The loader finds a tack.mod by walking up from the directory it is
// asked to build and reads it into a source.File, which parse takes as
// mut: it records the file's line starts, as the lexer does for a Tack
// file, so its diagnostics can be rendered, and gives the file back. It
// returns the ModFile the file declares, or every problem found with it.
//
// Go returns a *File or nil; here a ModFile is returned with a flag
// saying whether there is one. Go's CheckModule returns a message, empty
// for a valid name; check_module appends the message to a list and
// reports whether it did. show writes a name with a byte that is not
// printable ASCII Go-quoted, as Go's strconv.Quote does, through std's
// conv.append_quote.
//
// Every function that allocates returns the builtin append's out of
// memory as an error beside its result, which is of no use then.
